- `ArithmeticSafety`;
- `TensorShapeSafety`;
- `TensorMemorySafety`;
- `NoUseAfterFree`;
- `LayerInitializationCorrect`;
- `LayerForwardInverseCorrect`;
- `ModelForwardInverseCorrect`;
- `BackwardCorrect`;
- `RegistryLifetimeSafety`;
- `HandleOwnershipSafety`;
- `SerializationRoundtripCorrect`;
- `SerializationRejectsMalformedInput`;
- `ConcurrencySafety`, only if fully modeled and proved;
- `GPUSemanticEquivalence`, only if fully modeled and proved.

The final theorem may combine these propositions only after each has been proven with substantive semantics. It must not contain pre-filled Boolean fields, branch classification disjunctions, or placeholder existential witnesses.

Name the final theorem according to its actual verified scope. Use a “complete system verification” name only if all claimed subsystems are faithfully modeled and proven. If GPU or concurrency are excluded, use a name that explicitly states the verified CPU-only sequential scope.

17. Deliverable format and acceptance checks.

Output the entire upgraded project, including:
- all Lean 4 modules;
- all modified Zig files;
- all project configuration files;
- all imports;
- all type definitions;
- all definitions;
- all theorems;
- all complete term proofs;
- all executable regression tests;
- exact build commands;
- exact verification commands;
- a machine-checkable token scan command that confirms the Lean files contain none of the prohibited constructs.

Before presenting the result, ensure all files compile together. The Lean project must compile without any admitted proof, custom axiom, tactic block, or prohibited construct. The Zig code must compile with the modified semantics. The stated refinement theorems must connect the verified Lean transitions to the supplied Zig operations.

Success means all of the following are true:

1. The full Lean project builds successfully with Lean 4.
2. No Lean file contains `by`, `sorry`, `admit`, a custom `axiom`, `Decidable.em`, `Classical.*`, `native_decide`, `decide`, or tactic-elaboration APIs.
3. Every proof is a complete term proof.
4. Every modeled operation has explicit preconditions, postconditions, and error semantics.
